course: dictionary
lesson: 3287
title: Esq. and polite titles
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Esq.» is a short form, or abbreviation, of «Esquire». It is a polite title written after a man's name, mostly on letters, as in «John Smith, Esq.». People do not say it out loud. You will see it in writing, often on envelopes.
explain: «Esq.» is used instead of «Mr», never with it. So «Toad, Esq., Toad Hall» is correct, but «Mr Toad, Esq.» is not. In The Wind in the Willows, Toad is a gentleman, so his letters are addressed this way.
explain: In the USA, «Esq.» is also written after a lawyer's name, whether the lawyer is a man or a woman. In history, an esquire was a young man who served a knight, and later a gentleman just below a knight. Context shows you which meaning is being used.
fact: «Esq.» is short for «Esquire», and the full stop shows that the word is shortened.
fact: «Esq.» is a polite title written after a man's name, mostly on letters.
fact: «Toad, Esq., Toad Hall» is the address of Toad in The Wind in the Willows.
fact: In the USA, «Esq.» is used after the name of a lawyer, man or woman.
fact: Never write «Mr» and «Esq.» together for the same person.
exercise: What does «Esq.» stand for? | Estate | Express | *Esquire | Escape
exercise: In «Alice's Right Foot, Esq., Hearthrug, near the Fender», what is the hearthrug? | *The rug in front of the fireplace | A metal guard around the fireplace | A bed for a cat | A letter sent to a friend
exercise: Which address follows the rule of never using «Mr» and «Esq.» together? | Mr John Smith, Esq. | Mr Esq. John Smith | *John Smith, Esq. | John Smith, Mr Esq.
test: In the USA, after whose name might you see «Esq.»? | A teacher | A baker | *A lawyer | A farmer
test: Why is there a full stop in «Esq.»? | It means the letter is a joke | *It shows the word has been shortened | It makes the name sound older | It ends a sentence
exam: Which is the correct way to address a letter to a man called Jim Brown? | Esq. Mr Jim Brown | *Jim Brown, Esq. | Jim Brown, Mr Esq. | Mr Jim Brown, Esq.
